---
import ToolPage from '@versal/ui/ToolPage.astro';

const title = "File Checksum Calculator";
const description = "Compute the SHA-256, SHA-1, SHA-384, or SHA-512 checksum of any local file, with its size. Your file is processed locally and never uploaded.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "How do I compute a file checksum?",
    "answer": "Click Choose File (or drag and drop) to select any file. The tool computes MD5, SHA-1, SHA-256, SHA-384, and SHA-512 hashes entirely in your browser using the Web Crypto API. The file never leaves your device."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which hash algorithms are supported?",
    "answer": "MD5, SHA-1, SHA-256, SHA-384, and SHA-512 are all computed simultaneously. Note that MD5 and SHA-1 are considered weak for security purposes \u2014 use SHA-256 or stronger for cryptographic integrity checks."
  }
];
import ToolNav from '@versal/ui/ToolNav.astro';
import BaseLayout from '@versal/ui/BaseLayout';
---

<ToolPage
  site="sec"
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical="https://jsonversal.com/sec/tools/file-checksum/"
  current="/sec/tools/file-checksum/"
  faqs={faqs}
  about={[
    {
      "heading": "Fixed-length output, and what that tells you",
      "body": [
        "SHA-2 compresses input of any length into a fixed-width state: 160 bits for SHA-1, 256 for SHA-256, 384 and 512 for the wider variants. The digest length carries no information about the file — not its size, not how many bits differ, only whether the bytes match what you expected. In practice an accidental single-bit flip during transfer, a truncated download, or a stray line-ending conversion all produce a completely different digest with overwhelming probability.",
        "MD5 and SHA-1 stay available because release manifests, package indexes and legacy artifacts still reference them, and reproducing those values is a legitimate reason to compute them. Both have practical collision attacks against motivated adversaries, so they remain appropriate for detecting accidental corruption and inappropriate for anything adversarial — a checksum that an attacker can deliberately collide is not an integrity control."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "How the file is read, and what that costs",
      "body": [
        "The implementation calls `file.arrayBuffer()` and hands the entire result to `crypto.subtle.digest`, which is a one-shot API — Web Crypto has no streaming digest interface. Peak memory therefore tracks file size, and a multi-gigabyte disk image will throw a `RangeError` or kill the tab rather than hash. Note also that `crypto.subtle` only exists in a secure context: over plain `http://` on a non-localhost origin the whole tool is inert, which is a confusing failure if you are testing on a LAN address.",
        "For files too large to hold in memory, the workable approaches are an incremental implementation such as `hash-wasm`, or verifying on the machine that already holds the reference digest. When comparing text files across platforms, remember that the hash covers raw bytes — a UTF-8 BOM, CRLF line endings, or a trailing newline all change the digest, which is the usual explanation for a checksum that —should— match and does not."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Integrity is not authenticity, and not malware detection",
      "body": [
        "A checksum answers exactly one question: is this byte-identical to the value I was given? An attacker who controls the distribution point can publish a matching digest for a payload they modified, because the digest is not a signature. Closing that gap needs detached signatures — GPG, minisign, Sigstore — or a registry that signs what it serves, where the trust anchor is the key rather than the file.",
        "Nor does a checksum say anything about whether the content is malicious. Malware has whatever checksum the vendor published, because the checksum describes the file rather than judging it. Detection is a different job: antivirus engines, YARA rules, sandbox detonation. Use this page to answer —did the transfer corrupt or did I get the file I meant to get— and nothing beyond that."
      ]
    }
  ]}
>

  <section class="card mt-1">
    <h2 class="h2-card">Input</h2>
    <label class="text-muted" for="file">File</label>
    <label for="file" class="sr-only">Input</label> <input type="file" id="file" />
    <label class="text-muted" style="margin-top:12px;" for="algo">Algorithm</label>
    <label for="algo" class="sr-only">Input</label> <select id="algo">
      <option value="SHA-256">SHA-256 (recommended)</option>
      <option value="SHA-1">SHA-1</option>
      <option value="SHA-384">SHA-384</option>
      <option value="SHA-512">SHA-512</option>
    </select>
    <div class="row mt-2">
      <button class="primary" id="compute">Compute checksum</button>
    </div>
    <p class="text-dim" style="font-size:.875rem;margin:12px 0 0;">Your file is processed entirely in your local browser and is never uploaded to any server.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card mt-2">
    <strong>Checksum</strong>
    <pre id="sum-out" class="mt-2" style="display:none;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all;"></pre>
    <div id="meta" class="text-dim mt-2" style="display:none;"></div>
    <button class="ghost mt-1" id="copy" style="display:none;">Copy</button>
  </section>

  <section class="card mt-2">
    <strong>Error</strong>
    <div id="err" class="mt-2" style="display:none;"></div>
  </section>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The selected file is read into memory with <code>FileReader</code>/<code>arrayBuffer()</code> and hashed with <code>crypto.subtle.digest</code>.</li>
    <li>Each algorithm produces a fixed-length digest regardless of file size  — 160 bits for SHA-1, 256/384/512 bits for the SHA-2 family.</li>
    <li>The checksum is shown in hexadecimal, which is the format used by most download pages and package managers.</li>
    <li>Hashing runs in your browser only; the file never leaves your machine.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>About File Checksum Calculator</h2>
  <p class="text-muted">A checksum (or file hash) is a short fingerprint that changes drastically when even a single bit of the file changes. Its main uses are verifying that a downloaded file matches the publisher's official copy, detecting accidental corruption during transfer or storage, and deduplicating identical files. SHA-256 is the current recommendation  — it is fast, universally supported, and collision-resistant  — while SHA-1 is considered broken for security purposes and should only be used to match checksums published long ago.</p>
  <p class="text-muted">Use this tool whenever you download a binary, a firmware image, or an installer and want to confirm it was transferred intact. Keep in mind that a checksum only proves the file matches the value it was compared against; it does not prove the file is safe. A malicious actor who controls the download can simply publish a matching checksum  — for real protection against tampering, verify cryptographically signed hashes from a trusted source (for example, GPG signatures or HTTPS package registries).</p>
</ToolPage>

<style>
  .row { display: flex; gap: 8px; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  #err { color: var(--danger); }
</style>

<script is:inline>
(function () {
  const $ = id => document.getElementById(id);

  function toHex(buf) {
    return Array.from(new Uint8Array(buf)).map(b => b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('');
  }
  function fmtSize(bytes) {
    if (bytes < 1024) return bytes + ' B';
    if (bytes < 1024 * 1024) return (bytes / 1024).toFixed(1) + ' KB';
    if (bytes < 1024 * 1024 * 1024) return (bytes / (1024 * 1024)).toFixed(1) + ' MB';
    return (bytes / (1024 * 1024 * 1024)).toFixed(2) + ' GB';
  }

  $('compute').addEventListener('click', async () => {
    const f = $('file').files[0];
    const err = $('err');
    err.style.display = 'none';
    if (!f) { showToast('Please choose a file first.'); return; }

    const algo = $('algo').value;
    const btn = $('compute');
    const orig = btn.textContent;
    btn.disabled = true;
    btn.textContent = 'Hashing…';
    try {
      const buf = await f.arrayBuffer();
      const digest = await crypto.subtle.digest(algo, buf);
      $('sum-out').textContent = toHex(digest);
      $('sum-out').style.display = 'block';
      $('meta').textContent = 'File: ' + f.name + ' · Size: ' + fmtSize(f.size) + ' · Algorithm: ' + algo;
      $('meta').style.display = 'block';
      $('copy').style.display = 'inline-block';
    } catch (e) {
      err.textContent = 'Error: ' + e.message;
      err.style.display = 'block';
    } finally {
      btn.disabled = false;
      btn.textContent = orig;
    }
  });

  $('copy').addEventListener('click', async () => {
    try {
      await navigator.clipboard.writeText($('sum-out').textContent);
      $('copy').textContent = 'Copied!';
      setTimeout(() => { $('copy').textContent = 'Copy'; }, 1200);
    } catch {}
  });
})();
</script>
